proposals=[
(36,'14','Keep title and the first two bullets; replace the final bullet and question. Keep the renamer example.', ['UI: the controls, labels, and layout','UX: reaching the intended result in context','Define success before judging the interface'], 'What must change—and what must stay unchanged?', 'Think back to the renamer. The code can finish successfully while the artist still gets the wrong result. Name the intended change and what must remain untouched. Then ask how the interface makes those conditions visible.', '01–02, 05', 'The existing distinction is sound. Make success criteria visible before judging appearance.'),
(37,'14B','Keep title and user-story bullets; replace question and extend notes with context.', by_id['14B']['bullets'], 'What would the artist inspect to confirm success?', 'This story gives us a role, a goal and a benefit. Now add context: what is selected, which project is open, and what happens immediately before and after this task? A useful reference tool may solve a different problem. Borrow a pattern only when you can explain why it fits this artist.', '01–02, 05', 'Adds context without duplicating the existing user-story lesson.'),
(38,'14D','Keep title, illustration and its untested-sketch caption; replace question. Add this narration.', [], 'Which layout makes the target and next action easier to identify?', 'Both sketches can perform the same operation. That does not make them equally suitable for every artist. Predict which layout helps a new user find the target and which helps an experienced user repeat the task. Those are hypotheses. Watch someone use the sketch before calling either one better.', '03–05', 'Shows that technical function alone does not prescribe a single layout.'),
(39,'15','Keep title, existing material-preview capture and crop. Replace question and notes; add three short callouts beside the capture.', ['Target and scope','Proposed change','Next action'], 'Which information belongs together?', 'Find the target, then find what will change. The spacing and labels should help you connect those two pieces of information. A layout follows the task when it supports the artist’s decisions in context. That includes how they recognize controls, inspect consequences and recover—not just the order of our Python functions.', '04–05, 09–10', 'Adds a concrete visual-design reading while keeping colleagues’ material implementation outside this lesson.'),
(40,'14F','Keep title, GDC visual, credit and question. Add a compact three-label explanation; replace notes with this script.', ['Implementation: spawn and kill events','Artist’s goal: control VFX duration','Interface: a duration control'], by_id['14F']['question'], 'The implementation is what the system does internally. The artist’s mental model is how they understand the work. The interface represents that work. Here, duration brings two underlying events into one meaningful decision. Technical detail remains useful when it helps the artist make the next decision.', '05', 'The existing production example already teaches this well; the terms make the relationship explicit.'),
(44,'15B','Keep title, existing bullets, code excerpt and question. Add this explanation in notes; do not add a universal pixel rule.', by_id['15B']['bullets'], by_id['15B']['question'], 'A small interface still needs visible actions and understandable labels. Try the keyboard and look for focus. Check whether controls are easy to select in the actual window size. Mobile thumb-reach guidance does not directly describe a desktop Unreal panel. The accessible name shown here is one useful step; it is not a complete accessibility test.', '07–08', 'Transfers discoverability and input constraints without importing unsupported mobile measurements.'),
(46,'16E','Keep title, testing-loop visual and caption. Replace question; add the following narration.', [], 'Which single layout change would you test next? What questions do you have?', 'Suppose your tester cannot find the destination. That is an observation. Perhaps its label is unclear, or perhaps it is visually grouped with the wrong controls. Choose one explanation and make one change. Give the same task to a fresh tester and watch again. A cleaner-looking screen is a proposal until we have evidence about how it works.', '09–10, applied to the existing testing method', 'Converts visual preference into an observable test without claiming the Udemy examples are measured evidence.')
]
esc=lambda s:html.escape(str(s))
style='body{font:17px/1.6 system-ui;margin:0;background:#f3f5f8;color:#172536}main{max-width:1250px;margin:auto;padding:32px}h1,h2,h3{line-height:1.2}a{color:#17619a}header,article,section{background:white;padding:25px;margin:20px 0;border-radius:12px}article{border-top:6px solid #7752a4}.columns{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:25px}img{max-width:100%;height:auto}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;font:14px/1.6 system-ui;background:#f1f4f7;padding:15px}.tag{font-size:14px;color:#64517d}input{font:inherit;padding:10px;max-width:90%}blockquote{border-left:4px solid #7752a4;padding-left:18px;margin-left:0}@media(max-width:800px){.columns{grid-template-columns:1fr}}'
parts=[f'<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Week 7 · UI/UX integration review</title><style>{style}</style><main>', '<header><h1>UI/UX notes → Week 7</h1><p>14 October 2026 · Assessment and proposed copy. The PowerPoint has not been changed.</p><p><a href="organized-notes.md">Read the organized notes</a> · <a href="../UnrealFirst20261013/output/Week_7_Python_in_Unreal_v13.pptx">Current v13 deck</a> · <a href="../UnrealFirst20261013/index.html">Current deck review</a></p><p>Recommendation: enrich the existing UI/UX section and add one explicit grouping example. Preserve the Unreal-first opening, Blender handoff, colleague coverage and established schedule. These proposals are not constrained by estimated slide times.</p><p><strong>Source issue:</strong> lesson 06 is an exact duplicate of 04. The original ten files are preserved. Mobile dimensions, historical product opinions and unreferenced numerical claims are not carried into slide copy.</p><label>Find a topic <input id="search" placeholder="Try spacing, context, mental model"></label> <label><input id="route" type="checkbox"> Proposed live route only</label></header>', '<section><h2>Proposed live sequence</h2><p>35 section divider → 36 purpose → 37 user/context → 38 alternative layouts → 39 group the task → <a href="#new">39A spacing example</a> → 40 artist’s mental model → 41–44 state, implementation, recovery and accessibility → 45 production feedback → 46 test a prediction → existing peer-test preparation and labs.</p><p>Keep the section color and title transition. Use sublabels “Understand the task,” “Make the task visible,” and “Test the result” within this section. Architecture history and mobile-only examples remain in preparation notes because they are less directly relevant to desktop artist tools.</p></section>']
